To check active network ports in Ubuntu, use the built-in ss (socket statistics) command in your terminal.

1. List All Listening Ports & Programs

Run this command to see every open port waiting for connections along with the program using it:
bash
sudo ss -tulnp
Use code with caution.
• -t shows TCP ports.
• -u shows UDP ports.
• -l shows only listening (active) ports.
• -n shows raw port numbers (like 80 or 22) instead of service names.
• -p displays the Process ID (PID) and application name (requires sudo to view all apps).

2. Check a Specific Port

To see if a specific port (such as port 8080) is currently active, filter the output with grep:
bash
sudo ss -tulnp | grep :8080
Use code with caution.
• If it returns a line, that port is active and the output will show the program using it.
• If it returns nothing, the port is currently free and inactive.

3. View All Established Connections

If you want to see ports with active, ongoing data transfers rather than just waiting/listening ports, run:
bash
sudo ss -tunap
Use code with caution.
• Look for ESTAB in the State column to find live connections between your Ubuntu machine and external servers or devices.

4. Alternative Command (lsof)

If you prefer checking ports by file usage, you can also use lsof:
bash
sudo lsof -i -P -n | grep LISTEN
Use code with caution.
• Clear readability. This displays the application name, user, PID, and exact port number in clean columns.
